{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 37, :comments ["<b>Upon a cord of techelet.</b> And elsewhere (Exodus 39:31) it says <b>and they placed upon it a cord of techelet.</b> Moreover, it is written here <b>and it shall be upon the mitznefet</b>, and below it says <b>and it shall be upon Aaron's forehead</b>. And in the slaughtering of consecrated offerings (Zevachim 19a) we learned: his hair was visible between the tzitz and the mitznefet, for there he would place tefillin. From this we learn that the mitznefet is above, at the crown of the head — [it is not deeply set so as to receive the entire head down to the forehead] — and the tzitz is below. [And he would make three holes in it, at its two ends and in its middle], and the cords were in the holes, suspended from it at its two ends and in its middle — six in all, in these three places: one cord from above on the outside and one on the inside opposite it, and he ties the ends of the cords behind the nape of the neck, all three of them. The result is that between the length of the plate and the cords of its ends they encircle the crown, and the middle cord at its end is tied together with the ends of the two, running across the width of the head from above, so that it forms a kind of cap. And concerning the middle cord it says \"and it shall be upon the mitznefet,\" and he would place the tzitz on his head, and by means of the middle cord he supports it so it does not fall, and the plate hangs opposite his forehead. Thus all these verses are fulfilled: a cord upon the tzitz (Exodus 39:31), and the tzitz upon the cord (here), and the cord upon the mitznefet from above (here and there). All this is the language of Rashi (Rashi on Exodus 28:37).\n\nBut I am astonished at his words, for Scripture commands only to make a single cord of techelet, yet he makes six. Moreover, he combines the verses of the command and the execution, and multiplies cords to correspond to all of them — and so you could count likewise for the Ark and the Table and the Menorah. And if Scripture commanded that they place it upon a cord of techelet, how did they place upon it a cord of techelet — and where were they commanded to do so? And what need is there for the threads in the middle, when it is tied behind the nape of the neck in the manner of every tzitz worn by men and women?\n\nRather, the matter is not as the Rav said, and there was there only a single cord: the plate goes around from ear to ear, and is pierced at its two ends, and a cord of techelet is threaded through the two holes, and the tzitz is tied with it opposite the nape. Now, he would wrap the mitznefet around the head, not on the forehead at all, but above, in the place of the hair. And according to the opinion of our Sages (Zevachim 19a), he would also leave some of the hair of his head opposite his face for the tefillin. And the wrapping was at the crown of the head opposite the middle of the brain, and he covers the entire head with it opposite its back. So the wrapping is at the crown of the head opposite the brain, at an incline, and he places the tzitz opposite his forehead from ear to ear, and there is no interruption between his forehead and the tzitz. But from ear to ear, behind him opposite the entire nape, the mitznefet would be, and the cord that is tied in it would come upon it. And this is what it says: <b>and you shall place it upon a cord of techelet</b> — that he threads the cord through the holes of the tzitz, and the cord would be upon the mitznefet behind his ears opposite the nape; <b>opposite the face of the mitznefet it shall be</b> — the tzitz; <b>and it shall be upon Aaron's forehead</b> — and it shall be on the forehead, opposite the mitznefet on his face. And this is what it says (Exodus 29:6): <b>and you shall place the mitznefet upon his head</b> — that is to say, above, at the highest part of the head, not like the migba'ot, of which it is said (Exodus 29:9), <b>and you shall wrap migba'ot for them</b>, which wrap the head like one who has a headache and wraps it for support. And it says (Exodus 29:6): <b>and you shall place the nezer of holiness upon the mitznefet</b>, for upon it he ties it from behind. And likewise it says (Exodus 39:31): <b>and they placed upon it a cord of techelet, to place upon the mitznefet from above</b>, for there he would fasten the knot. And likewise: <b>and he placed upon the mitznefet, opposite his face, the tzitz of gold</b> (Leviticus 8:9) — that he placed, in its tying, upon the mitznefet, and opposite his face was the tzitz of gold. Thus the placing upon the mitznefet in all the verses refers to the tying, but the tzitz is opposite the face of the mitznefet, on the forehead.\n\nAnd one should not be stringent between <b>and they placed upon it a cord of techelet</b> (Exodus 39:31) and <b>and you shall place it upon a cord of techelet</b> (here), for in both it is as if it says \"and you shall place it in a cord of techelet,\" and \"they placed in it a cord of techelet,\" in the manner of <b>and you shall place them upon one basket</b> (Exodus 29:3) — in a basket — and similar to it: <b>deliver yourself for your life</b> (Genesis 19:17). Or it may serve in place of אֶל: וְנָתַתָּ אֹתוֹ אֶל פְּתִיל תְּכֵלֶת, וַיִּתְּנוּ אֵלָיו פְּתִיל תְּכֵלֶת. For the cord is placed to it, and it is upon the cord, for upon it does it rest.\n\nThe general principle is that the intent of the verses is only that they thread the cord through the holes of the tzitz, and so too it is that the tzitz is upon the cord and the cord is upon the tzitz. And likewise <b>and they shall bind the breastpiece from its rings to the rings of the ephod with a cord of techelet</b> (Exodus 28:28), which Rashi brought as proof from it — it is a single cord: they would thread one cord through the rings of the breastpiece and through the rings of the ephod opposite them, and likewise at the other end a single cord, for these are two places and at each they bind it with a single cord."], 